1 NOTE My father, Wilford Wayne Kimball is the son of, Wiilford Alfonzo who is the son of
2 CONT HEBER C .KIMBALL
2 CONT Heber Chase Kimball was born 14 June 1801 in Sheldon, Franklin County, Vermont.
2 CONT He writes the following pertaining to his baptism;
2 CONT "One day in April, while working in his shop (he was a potter by trade) Alpheus
2 CONT Gifford entered and Heber C. Kimball said to him, Brother Alpheus, I am ready to
2 CONT go forward and be baptized." He then writes: "I arose, pulled off my apron, washed
2 CONT my hands and started with him, with my sleeves rolled up to my shoulders, and went
2 CONT a distance of one mile, where he baptized me in a small stream in the woods. After
2 CONT I was baptized I kneeled down and he laid his hands upon my head and confirmed me
2 CONT a member of the Church of Jesus Christ, and said unto me, "In the name of Jesus
2 CONT Christ, and by the authority of the Holy Priesthood, receive ye the Holy Ghost,and
2 CONT before I got up off my knees he wanted to ordain me an Elder: but I pleaded with
2 CONT him not to do it, for I felt myself unworthy of such a calling, and such an office."
2 CONT "Under the ordinances of baptism and the laying on of hands, I received the Holy
2 CONT Ghost, as the desciples did in ancient days, which was like a consuming fire. I felt
2 CONT as if I sat at the feet of Jesus , and was clothed in my right mind, although the
2 CONT people called me crazy.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I continued in this way for many months, and it seemed as though my body would
2 CONT consume away; at the same time the scriptures were unfolded to my mind in such a
2 CONT wonderful manner that it appeared to me, at times, as if I had formerly been familiar with them ." The date of his baptism was 15 April 1832.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 13, 1837 Heber C. along with Elders Orson Hyde, Willard Richards, and Joseph Fielding, started without purse or script on the first foreign mission for the Church.  He writes that after obtaining eighteen dollars each, the
2 CONC y were able to obtain a few provisions, and were ready to board the new packet ship Garrick, of 900 tons, for Liverpool, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Smithies - (from writings of Heber C. Kimball)
2 CONT
2 CONT "I will mention a circumstance in relation to the first child born in the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day-Saints in Great Britain, which was on the 7th of October 1837 at Barshe Lees.  She was the daughter of James and Nancy 
2 CONC Smithies.  After she was born her parents wanted to take her to the church to be sprinkled, or christened, as they called it.  I used every kind of persuasion to convince them of their folly; it being contrary to the scriptures an
2 CONC d the will of God; the parents wept bitterly, and it seemed as if I could not prevail on them to omit it.  I wanted to know of them why they were so tenacious.  The answer was, if she dies she cannot bave burial in the churchyard.
2 CONC   I said to them, Brother and Sister Smithies, I say unto ;you in the name of Israiel's God, she shall not die on this land, for she shall live until she becomes a mother in Israel, and I say it in the name of Jesus Christ and by 
2 CONC virtue of the Holy Priesthood vested in me.  I took it in my arms and blessed it, that it should live to become a mother in Israel.  She was the first child blessed in that country, and the first born unto them."
2 CONT
2 CONT The child was Mary Smithies.  She grew to wormanhood, emigrating with her parents to America and became Heber's wife and the mother of five of his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilford Alfonzo Kimball, third child of Heber C. Kimball and Mary Smithies was born Tuesday October 6, 1863 at 4:30 P.M. in Salt Lake City, Utah.  He was baptized by Wilford Woodruff Jr. 10 September 1876.  December 16, 1885 he wa
2 CONC s married and sealed to Ella Louise Free in the Logan Temple by Apostle Merrill.  They were blessed with seven children, six of whom lived to raise families.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a farmer and also worked for the County Street Department. He was interested in civic and church affairs and was a Bishop's Counselor for many years.  He will be remembered by his children and friends for his faith and char
2 CONC ity to those less fortunate than himself.  All of his posterity who have married, with the exception of two, have been married and sealed in the Temple.  He passed away 15 November 1928 and his posterity today number well over a h
2 CONC undred.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilford Wayne Kimball, seventh child of Wilford Alfonzo Kimball and Ella Louise Free was born 9 April 1903 in Salt Lake City, Utah.  He was Baptized 6 May 1911.  He was married in the Salt Lake Temple 6 June 1928 to Ruth Burton, b
2 CONC y Apostle George F. Richards.  They were blessed with five children.  Two daughters and three sons.  All three sons have completed full term missions.  Four of the five children are at present married.  All have been married in th
2 CONC e Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilford Wayne Kimball served in the Bishopric for fourteen years under four different Bishops.  He was the Bishop of the Richards Ward for four years from  June 1st, 1961.  He was sustained as Stake President of Sugarhouse Stake. 
2 CONC  He has 16 grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia Kimball, second child of Wilford Wayne Kimball and Ruth Burton was born 20 May 1933 in Salt Lake City, Utah.  Baptized 24 Jun 1941.  Married in the Salt Lake Temple 15 November 1951, to Raymond Keith Johnson, by Apostle S
2 CONC pencer W. Kimball.  At present they have five children.  (July 1965)
2 CONT
2 CONT Heber Chase Kimball was a member of the Presidency when Brigham Young was the President of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day-Saints.
1 NOTE My father, Wilford Wayne Kimball is the son of, Wiilford Alfonzo who is the son of
2 CONT HEBER C .KIMBALL
2 CONT Heber Chase Kimball was born 14 June 1801 in Sheldon, Franklin County, Vermont.
2 CONT He writes the following pertaining to his baptism;
2 CONT "One day in April, while working in his shop (he was a potter by trade) Alpheus
2 CONT Gifford entered and Heber C. Kimball said to him, Brother Alpheus, I am ready to
2 CONT go forward and be baptized." He then writes: "I arose, pulled off my apron, washed
2 CONT my hands and started with him, with my sleeves rolled up to my shoulders, and went
2 CONT a distance of one mile, where he baptized me in a small stream in the woods. After
2 CONT I was baptized I kneeled down and he laid his hands upon my head and confirmed me
2 CONT a member of the Church of Jesus Christ, and said unto me, "In the name of Jesus
2 CONT Christ, and by the authority of the Holy Priesthood, receive ye the Holy Ghost,and
2 CONT before I got up off my knees he wanted to ordain me an Elder: but I pleaded with
2 CONT him not to do it, for I felt myself unworthy of such a calling, and such an office."
2 CONT "Under the ordinances of baptism and the laying on of hands, I received the Holy
2 CONT Ghost, as the desciples did in ancient days, which was like a consuming fire. I felt
2 CONT as if I sat at the feet of Jesus , and was clothed in my right mind, although the
2 CONT people called me crazy.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I continued in this way for many months, and it seemed as though my body would
2 CONT consume away; at the same time the scriptures were unfolded to my mind in such a
2 CONT wonderful manner that it appeared to me, at times, as if I had formerly been familiar with them ." The date of his baptism was 15 April 1832.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 13, 1837 Heber C. along with Elders Orson Hyde, Willard Richards, and Joseph Fielding, started without purse or script on the first foreign mission for the Church.  He writes that after obtaining eighteen dollars each, the
2 CONC y were able to obtain a few provisions, and were ready to board the new packet ship Garrick, of 900 tons, for Liverpool, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Smithies - (from writings of Heber C. Kimball)
2 CONT
2 CONT "I will mention a circumstance in relation to the first child born in the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day-Saints in Great Britain, which was on the 7th of October 1837 at Barshe Lees.  She was the daughter of James and Nancy 
2 CONC Smithies.  After she was born her parents wanted to take her to the church to be sprinkled, or christened, as they called it.  I used every kind of persuasion to convince them of their folly; it being contrary to the scriptures an
2 CONC d the will of God; the parents wept bitterly, and it seemed as if I could not prevail on them to omit it.  I wanted to know of them why they were so tenacious.  The answer was, if she dies she cannot bave burial in the churchyard.
2 CONC   I said to them, Brother and Sister Smithies, I say unto ;you in the name of Israiel's God, she shall not die on this land, for she shall live until she becomes a mother in Israel, and I say it in the name of Jesus Christ and by 
2 CONC virtue of the Holy Priesthood vested in me.  I took it in my arms and blessed it, that it should live to become a mother in Israel.  She was the first child blessed in that country, and the first born unto them."
2 CONT
2 CONT The child was Mary Smithies.  She grew to wormanhood, emigrating with her parents to America and became Heber's wife and the mother of five of his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilford Alfonzo Kimball, third child of Heber C. Kimball and Mary Smithies was born Tuesday October 6, 1863 at 4:30 P.M. in Salt Lake City, Utah.  He was baptized by Wilford Woodruff Jr. 10 September 1876.  December 16, 1885 he wa
2 CONC s married and sealed to Ella Louise Free in the Logan Temple by Apostle Merrill.  They were blessed with seven children, six of whom lived to raise families.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a farmer and also worked for the County Street Department. He was interested in civic and church affairs and was a Bishop's Counselor for many years.  He will be remembered by his children and friends for his faith and char
2 CONC ity to those less fortunate than himself.  All of his posterity who have married, with the exception of two, have been married and sealed in the Temple.  He passed away 15 November 1928 and his posterity today number well over a h
2 CONC undred.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilford Wayne Kimball, seventh child of Wilford Alfonzo Kimball and Ella Louise Free was born 9 April 1903 in Salt Lake City, Utah.  He was Baptized 6 May 1911.  He was married in the Salt Lake Temple 6 June 1928 to Ruth Burton, b
2 CONC y Apostle George F. Richards.  They were blessed with five children.  Two daughters and three sons.  All three sons have completed full term missions.  Four of the five children are at present married.  All have been married in th
2 CONC e Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilford Wayne Kimball served in the Bishopric for fourteen years under four different Bishops.  He was the Bishop of the Richards Ward for four years from  June 1st, 1961.  He was sustained as Stake President of Sugarhouse Stake. 
2 CONC  He has 16 grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia Kimball, second child of Wilford Wayne Kimball and Ruth Burton was born 20 May 1933 in Salt Lake City, Utah.  Baptized 24 Jun 1941.  Married in the Salt Lake Temple 15 November 1951, to Raymond Keith Johnson, by Apostle S
2 CONC pencer W. Kimball.  At present they have five children.  (July 1965)
