1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://www.lds.org/churchhistory/library/pioneerdetails/1,15791,4018-1-569,00.html  	Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel, 1847–1868 Charles C. Rich Company (1847)]  Age 14
2 CONT
2 CONT Departure: 21 June 1847 Arrival: 2 October 1847
2 CONT
2 CONT Company Information: 126 individuals were in the company when it began its journey from the outfitting post on the Elkhorn River about 27 miles west of Winter Quarters, Nebraska.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Sanford Porter and Nancy Areta Warner
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Electa Mariah Kilbourn, 5 Nov 1852, Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Sarah Catherine Emmett, 18 Mar 1865, Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Elizabeth Bailey, 5 Oct 1867, Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT History - Lyman Wight Porter, a Patriarch in the LDS Church and a Utah pioneer of 1847, was born May 5, 1833, in Jackson County, Missouri, the son of Sanford Porter.  With his parents he shared in the persecutions of the Saints, b
2 CONC eing driven from Jackson County, later from Clay County and still later from Caldwell County, Missouri.  He located with his parents at Montrose, Iowa, where he was baptized in 1841.  At the time of the exodus of the Saints from N
2 CONC auvoo, in 1846, he traveled with his people to the Missouri river and spent the winter of 1846-1847 at Winter Quarters.  In 1847 he continued the journey to Great Salt Lake Valley, crossing the plains in the Charles C. Rich's ccom
2 CONC pany.  In 1848 the family located in Mill Creek, Salt Lake County, but moved to Salt Lake City soon afterwards.  In 1851, with his father's family, Lyman moved to Centerville, Davis County, which becam his home for a number of yea
2 CONC rs.  While residing there he took part in the Indian troubles, and in 1857 accompanied Orrin P. Rockwell to the mountains to watch the movements of Johnston's army.  In 1856 he was called to go with others to help the belated hand
2 CONC cart companies into the Valley.  In 1862 he settled in Porterville, Morgan county.  In 1867 he was appointed to preside over the Porterville branch and under his direction the East Porterville townsite was laid out.  He made the f
2 CONC irst brick and built the first brick house in Morgan county.  In the spring of 1869 he resigned his position as president of the branch to go to Missouri to assist in bringing his kinspeople to Utah.  Returning by way of Chicago, 
2 CONC he purchased wagons, machinery and cattle.  In 1880 he was chosen as a member of the High Council of the Morgan Stake and later ordained a Patriarch.  He also acted as sheriff, selectman, road commisioner, etc., in Morgan County. 
2 CONC  Leaving a numerous posterity, Patriarch Porter died March 31, 1914, in Porterville."
2 CONT
2 CONT Courtesy of LDS Biographical Encyclopedia, Andrew Jenson, Vol. 3, p. 491. Note: Helped Save the Handcart Pioneers in 1856
