1 NAME Betty Lucille /Jones/
2 GIVN Betty Lucille
2 SURN Jones
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 15 FEB 1930
1 DEAT
2 DATE 26 JUL 2014
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Our loving, sweet mother, Betty Lucille Jones Walker, died Saturday, July 26, 2014, from complications of Alzheimer's disease, which she fought with great courage, all while maintaining a happy and kind disposition.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born February 15, 1930, to Lewis Howell Jones and Lucille Reeves, and was the oldest of four children. She was raised in Brigham City, Utah, and relished her joyful childhood memories. She loved living directly across the 
2 CONC street from her Grandpa Jones, a widower, who would put her on his knee and let her pick out anything she wanted in the Sears catalog. She paid frequent visits to her loving Grandma and Grandpa Reeves on Third East. She loved grow
2 CONC ing up in her childhood neighborhood, traveling to California with her parents on the train, and attending family reunions. Due to her father's military assignment during WWII, she moved to Hawaii during her junior year and gradua
2 CONC ted from high school there. While in Hawaii she took private hula lessons on Waikiki Beach and entertained everyone with her hula dancing.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 22, 1949, she married Max Walker in Brigham City. Their marriage was later solemnized in the Salt Lake City LDS Temple. They were married 51 years and shared many wonderful memories and made many lasting friendships during
2 CONC  their life together while living in Salt Lake City, Cedar City, Tremonton, and Brigham City, Utah, where they eventually retired. Max passed away in December of 2000.
2 CONT
2 CONT Betty was a devoted member of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints and loved the Brigham City 13th Ward and all her friends and neighbors. She had a kind and compassionate heart, was always thinking of others, and servi
2 CONC ng wherever she saw a need. She enjoyed good movies, traveling, reading, and spending time with friends and family. She loved playing tennis and offered a reward to any grandchild who could beat her—none did! She loved her grandch
2 CONC ildren and was proud of all of them. Her missionary grandchildren received more letters and newspaper clippings from Grandma Walker than anyone!
2 CONT
2 CONT She has left us a legacy of faith, service, compassion and love. We look forward to being reunited with Mom and Dad again. Families are forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is survived by her four children, Wendi (Doug) Ficklin, currently serving as full-time missionaries in the Sacramento, California LDS mission; Allyson Ollivier, (Midway), Lezli (John) Gunn (Ogden) and Mark (Cathi) Walker (Brig
2 CONC ham City); 13 grandchildren, Brandon, Aimee, Courtnie, Jon, Ryan, Jeff, Paul, Greg, Ginger, Brooke, Lyndsi, McKenzie and Sadie; 24 great grandchildren; two sisters, Ann Bryant and Patricia Munson and one brother, Lewis Jones, Jr.;
2 CONC  sister-in-law Shirley Haynie and brother-in-law Leo Walker.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family wishes to thank Dr. Lynn Beard and Richard Hall for their kind and compassionate medical care. The family also expresses a special thank-you to her dear friends, Deon Nielson and Lynn and Linda Garrett for their never e
2 CONC nding love and devoted friendship to her. We appreciate all those in the Memory Care Unit at Legacy House of Ogden, who provided attentive, compassionate care during her time there.
2 CONT
2 CONT A viewing for family and friends will be held Saturday, August 2, from 9:00-10:30 a.m. at the Brigham City Thirteenth Ward, 25 North 300 East.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral will follow at 11:00 a.m. in the chapel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Interment will be in the Brigham City Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT In lieu of flowers, please donate to the Missionary Fund of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints. 
2 CONT
