1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * '''Residence''': Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah, United States
2 CONT * '''Residence''': Utah, United States - 1870
2 CONT * '''Residence''': ED 26 Precinct 25 Salt Lake City Ward 2, Salt Lake, Utah, United States - 1900
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/KWJH-TB7
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Ashton was born 22 August 1821 in Caersws, Montgomeryshire, Wales, an ancient city founded by the Romans, in a financial and morally exhausted time. His father Richard died in 1824 leaving his wife Elizabeth with three smal
2 CONC l children in destitute condition. Edward was only eight years old when he was employed in a home factory from 6 a.m. to 9 p.m. and was paid three pence a day (five cents). When he was ten years old his right arm was badly torn an
2 CONC d he was crippled and unable to work for some time. He secured a job to do chores around the house of a good family and received his board and clothing for two years. At age 12 he was apprenticed to a shoemaker and was "bound soli
2 CONC d" for three to four years. He was abused and beaten and treated like a slave. He was hurt badly but able to run away; a stranger rescued him. The apprenticeship was severed as Edward was half starved and beaten.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward's mother married again - to Evan Morgan Kinsey of Llanwnog, Montgomeryshire, Wales. Edward didn't like the man so he left home. He worked as a shoemaker among a dozen mean, dishonest men playing cards and gambling. He gathe
2 CONC red enough money to make a visit home and was received with gladness by his mother. He returned to Tredegar and worked for a kind man named Ellis for ten years. He met the Mormon missionaries when he was very much troubled about r
2 CONC eligion. He was baptized in 1849, age 28, and in 1850 he sailed to America aboard the Joseph Badger ship of emigrants. He sailed to St. Louis and the dreaded cholera struck. He was very ill; some of the travelers died. He arrived 
2 CONC in Salt Lake in September 1852 in the last remnant of wagon teams.
2 CONT
2 CONT He found work as a shoemaker for John Taylor's family where he became acquainted with Jane Treharne. They married on 6 February 1854. Edward and Jane had seven children: Edward Treharne , Jedediah William, Brigham Willard, Elizabe
2 CONC th Ann, Sarah Jane, Emily Treharne and George Savage residing in the Fifteenth Ward. Edward served as a captain of ten in 1857 when Brigham Young sent the militia to Echo Canyon when Johnson's army came through the Valley. He took
2 CONC  his family 60 miles south as instructed by Brigham Young. He was one of the guards and lay on the ground most of the day as the army passed by. Edward was called on to fight the Indians several times. He died peacefully at home o
2 CONC n 7 February 1904 at age 83. 
