1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=137196 Find a Grave]
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 	Nov. 23, 1823 Lawrenceville Lawrence County Illinois, USA Death: 	Oct. 17, 1901 Farmington Davis County Utah, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Timothy Baldwin Clark and Mary Keeler
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Mary Stevenson, 18 May 1845, Clark Settlement, Lee, Iowa
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Susan Leggett, 8 Nov 1861, Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Nancy Areta Porter, 11 Jul 1870, Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT History - Clark, Ezra Thompson, a Patriarch, president of the High Priests' quorum of the Davis Stake of Zion and a resident of Farmington, Davis county, Utah, was born at Lawrenceville, Lawrence county, Illinois, Nov. 23, 1823, t
2 CONC he son of Timothy B. Clark and Mary Keeler. He was baptized by Wm. O. Clark in Fishing River, Clay county, Missouri, in March, 1836, and was with the Saints during the mobbings of Missouri and Illinois. In 1845 he was ordained a S
2 CONC eventy and became a member of the 19th quorum of Seventy. He left Nauvoo as an exile together with the rest of the Saints in 1846, and reached Great Salt Lake Valley in 1848. Together with a few other settlers he spent the first w
2 CONC inter near the mouth of North Canyon in Davis county, about one mile south of Bountiful, and located in 1849 at Farmington, where he resided during there remainder of his life. When he crossed the plains in 1848 his earthly posses
2 CONC sions amounted to a very little, but by industry and hard labor he succeeded in acquiring considerable property. In 1856-1858 he filled a successful mission to Great Britain. In the year 1869 he went to the States after the remain
2 CONC s of his son, Ezra James, who had died the year before while returning from a mission to Great Britain. In 1869-1870 he filled a short mission to the States, and in 1876 he labored as a missionary in Oregon and California. When th
2 CONC e Davis Stake of Zion was organized in 1877 he was chosen as a member of the High Council. Responding to a call from the Church authorities he assisted in locating the settlement of Georgetown, Bear Lake county, Idaho. Having yiel
2 CONC ded obedience to the principle of plural marriage he served a short term in the Utah penitentiary, having been convicted under the provisions of the Edmunds law of so-called unlawful co-habitation. He was ordained a Patriarch abou
2 CONC t 1895. Elder Clark died at Farmington Oct. 17, 1901, leaving a large posterity. He was a natural financier and spent his means liberally in the interest of the Church, and not only did he fill a number of missions himself, but he
2 CONC  also assisted a number of his sons to fill successful missions to different parts of the world. Bro. Clark married three wives. By his first wife, Mary Stevenson, he had eleven children, namely, Ezra James (who died in the missio
2 CONC nary field ), Timothy Baldwin, Mary Elizabeth, William Henry, Joseph Smith, Hyrum Don Carlos, Edward Barrett, Charles Rich, Wilford Woodruff, Amasa Lyman and David Patten. By his second wife, Susan Leggett, he had ten children, na
2 CONC mely, Seymour Thompson, Anna Vilate, Sarah Lovina, Susan Alice Bell, John Alexander (who died in the missionary field in Palestine), Eugene Henry, Nathan George, Marion Franklin, Laura Blanch and Horace Wells. Brother Clark's thir
2 CONC d wife, Nancy Porter, had no children by Bro. Clark. Bro. Clark is the only man in the Church known to have lost two sons by death in the missionary field.
2 CONT
2 CONT LDS Biographical Encyclopedia, Andrew Jenson, Vol. 3, p. 343 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links: 
