1 NAME Øystein Sondreson /Bakkejordet/
2 GIVN Øystein Sondreson
2 SURN Bakkejordet
2 _MARNM Sanderson
2 NICK Øystein
2 NICK Østen
2 NICK Osten
2 NICK Bakka
1 NAME Øystein Sanderson (Bakkejordet)
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 NOV 1789
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bakkajord
3 STAE Telemark
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE DEC 1837
2 ADDR
3 CITY Beaver Creek Settlement
3 STAE Illinois
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Found this website about the Beaver Creek Tradegy.  Very interesting.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.xmission.com/~rhubarb/bcrk-tragedy.htm  - link broken
2 CONT
2 CONT update: https://sites.google.com/site/sandersx2/Home/beavercreektragedy
2 CONT
2 CONT Spoke with the author and he emailed me an electronic version of the book.  I uploaded so anyone who would like can download it and read through it.  You can find it here:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.sandhillmedia.net/osten_sondresson.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT -Chad Sanders
2 CONT
2 CONT chad@sandhillmedia.net
2 CONT
2 CONT Osten Sondreson was born November 8, 1789 on Bakka farm in Tinn, Telemark, Norway. He was the 5th child of Sondre Gjermundson and Margit Oysteinson. As patronymics was the custom in Norway he was known by the surname of Sondreson.
2 CONC  Hence today we have the name of Sanders as an anglicize version.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bakkajord, Tinn, Telemark, Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Osten married Aase Olson, from Romeraasen farm in Tinn, in 1815. They had nine children, two of which died at the age of three. Osten and Aase left their native land in the spring of 1837 for America. https://www.nb.no/items/232be
2 CONC e7b6c9b982eb8f68a56462c129f?page=317&searchText=%22%C3%B8ystein%20sondresen%22 They traveled to Goteborg, Sweden and obtained passage on the brig "Nord", commanded by Captain H. P. Brunk. They sailed for American sometime in May o
2 CONC f 1837 and according to the ships manifest they arrived in New York harbor on August 15, 1837.
2 CONT
2 CONT They traveled by canal boat and other means to Chicago. There they met other Norwegian immigrants and rather than proceed to the Fox River Settlements in LaSalle County, where the Norwegian settlements were located, they opted to 
2 CONC go with a number of other families and settle on some land which they called Beaver Creek. This was located about seventy-five miles south of Chicago on the Illinois and Indiana border. It was in Iroquois County near the present t
2 CONC own of Watseka, Illinois. Osten and Aase traveled to America with Osten's brother-in-law, Erik Gauteson Midtboen Haugen and his family. On October 7, 1837 his brother-in-law filed, with the Land Abstract Office, a claim for 160 ac
2 CONC res of land. We assume that Osten and Aase were very close by and that perhaps the land claim was filed upon jointly for both families by Erik.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the first winter of 1837/38 Osten, Aase, and at least one child died in the Beaver Creek Settlement. There are a number of historic accounts of this settlement that are available for referencing. The malaria epidemic was th
2 CONC e cause of most of the deaths of Beaver Creek Settlement. Because of the epidemic the settlement was abandoned early in 1838.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Sondreson surviving orphans were farmed out to various families and at least four of them were located, by my research, in the Fox River Settlements. They were living with various families. I have tried to locate all of the or
2 CONC phans and have been able to located only four. Kari was blind and lived with a family until her death after 1860. Another child, Ole or George as he called himself in America, reportedly migrated to California in the 1860's and no
2 CONC thing is known of him after that time. It has been thought that there were perhaps two of the children died in Beaver Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of the orphans, Ellen, Harriet, and Sondra, eventually joined the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints (Mormon) and came separately to the Salt Lake Valley as early pioneers. Ellen and Harriet. along with their brothe
2 CONC r Sondre, traveled to a church conference in Nauvoo, Illinois a few months after the martyrdom of the Prophet Joseph Smith. Ellen and Harriet stayed in Nauvoo and Sondre returned to Fox River.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ellen and Harriet both married Apostle Heber C. Kimball in January of 1846. Heber C. Kimball chose Ellen to accompany him in the first pioneer company which arrived in the Great Salt Lake Valley in July of 1847. She was one of the
2 CONC  three women in the first company and her image has been placed on the "This Is The Place Monument" in Emigration Canyon east of Salt Lake City. Harriet came to Salt Lake a year or so later. Sondra, my great grandfather, arrived i
2 CONC n Salt Lake in 1850.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much of the history of Beaver Creek and the history of the Sanders ancestors comes from a written history of Sondre Sanders Sr. who dictated it prior to his death. Details of this history have been incorporated in a family history
2 CONC  book , "From The Land Of The Midnight Sun-Osten and Aase Sondreson from Bakkajord", which I had published in 1992. Copies of this book are found in the Salt Lake Family History Library, Ogden Utah Family History Library, Brigham 
2 CONC Young University Library, L.D.S. Church Historians Office, and the Iroquois County Historical Society Library in Watseka, Illinois. 
