1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Daughter of David Sessions and Patty Bartlett
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Windsor Palmer Lyon, Mar 1838, Orwell, Addison, Vermont. He died Jan 1849, Iowa City, Johnson, Iowa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children - Diana Lyon, Marion Lyon, Philofreen Lyon, Asa Windsor Lyon, Josephine Rosetta Lyon, Byron Windsor Lyon, David Carlos Lyon, Charles William Lyon
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Joseph Smith, February 8, 1842
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Heber Chase Kimball, 26 Jan 1846, Nauvoo, Hancock, Illinois
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Ezekiel Clark, 1 Jan 1850, Iowa City, Johnson, Iowa. He died 23 Jun 1898, Iowa City, Johnson, Iowa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children - Perry Ezekial Clark, Phebe Jane Clark, Martha Sylvia Clark
2 CONT
2 CONT History - Sylvia Sessions left Maine for Zion (Missouri) with her parents, Patty and David, in June of 1837.  While in Missouri, Sylvia met and married Windsor Lyon.  Sylvia's Mother, Patty, wrote about the wedding in her journal,
2 CONC  "Sylvia was married to Windsor P. Lyon, Joseph Smith performed the ceremony... The next day the Prophet was there and a good time it was." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sylvia, and husband Windsor, left Missouri for Nauvoo in February of 1839.  There, Windsor established a mercantile business, selling "Dry Goods, Groceries, Crockery, Glass, and Hardware, Drugs, and Medicines, Paints and Dry Stuff
2 CONC s."  By this time, they were the parents of two children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sylvia married Joseph Smith on February 8, 1842, when she was 23 years old.  It is uncertain if her husband, Windsor, was aware of the marriage, but she did continue to live with him.  Brigham Young taught that "if the woman prefe
2 CONC rred a man higher in authority, and he is willing to take her and her husband gives her up-there is no Bill of divorce required...it is right in the sight of God". Brigham also explained that the woman, "...would be in a higher gl
2 CONC ory".  This may help shed light on Sylvia's complex marriage arrangement.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10 months later, on December 24th, Joseph's journal mentions a visit to his wife, Sylvia, who was giving birth to her third child: "Walked with Secretary Willard Richards] to see Sister Lyons who was sick. Her baby died 30 minutes
2 CONC  before [we] arrived".  Sylvia had lost two of her three children in death.  On September 18, 1843, another of Joseph's visits to Sylvia is recorded by William Clayton, "Joseph and I rode out to borrow money, drank wine at Sister 
2 CONC Lyons P.M. I got $50 of Sister Lyons and paid it to D. D. Yearsly."
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 27, 1844 her only surviving child, Philofreen, also died.
2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=43324160 Find A Grave Memorial] via mother [http://www.geni.com/profile-34670496297 Patty  Sessions (born Bartlett)] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartC
2 CONC opy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Feb 25 2015, 19:14:00 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Daughter of David Sessions and Patty Bartlett
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Windsor Palmer Lyon, Mar 1838, Orwell, Addison, Vermont. He died Jan 1849, Iowa City, Johnson, Iowa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children - Diana Lyon, Marion Lyon, Philofreen Lyon, Asa Windsor Lyon, Josephine Rosetta Lyon, Byron Windsor Lyon, David Carlos Lyon, Charles William Lyon
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Joseph Smith, February 8, 1842
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Heber Chase Kimball, 26 Jan 1846, Nauvoo, Hancock, Illinois
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Ezekiel Clark, 1 Jan 1850, Iowa City, Johnson, Iowa. He died 23 Jun 1898, Iowa City, Johnson, Iowa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children - Perry Ezekial Clark, Phebe Jane Clark, Martha Sylvia Clark
2 CONT
2 CONT History - Sylvia Sessions left Maine for Zion (Missouri) with her parents, Patty and David, in June of 1837.  While in Missouri, Sylvia met and married Windsor Lyon.  Sylvia's Mother, Patty, wrote about the wedding in her journal,
2 CONC  "Sylvia was married to Windsor P. Lyon, Joseph Smith performed the ceremony... The next day the Prophet was there and a good time it was." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sylvia, and husband Windsor, left Missouri for Nauvoo in February of 1839.  There, Windsor established a mercantile business, selling "Dry Goods, Groceries, Crockery, Glass, and Hardware, Drugs, and Medicines, Paints and Dry Stuff
2 CONC s."  By this time, they were the parents of two children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sylvia married Joseph Smith on February 8, 1842, when she was 23 years old.  It is uncertain if her husband, Windsor, was aware of the marriage, but she did continue to live with him.  Brigham Young taught that "if the woman prefe
2 CONC rred a man higher in authority, and he is willing to take her and her husband gives her up-there is no Bill of divorce required...it is right in the sight of God". Brigham also explained that the woman, "...would be in a higher gl
2 CONC ory".  This may help shed light on Sylvia's complex marriage arrangement.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10 months later, on December 24th, Joseph's journal mentions a visit to his wife, Sylvia, who was giving birth to her third child: "Walked with Secretary Willard Richards] to see Sister Lyons who was sick. Her baby died 30 minutes
2 CONC  before [we] arrived".  Sylvia had lost two of her three children in death.  On September 18, 1843, another of Joseph's visits to Sylvia is recorded by William Clayton, "Joseph and I rode out to borrow money, drank wine at Sister 
2 CONC Lyons P.M. I got $50 of Sister Lyons and paid it to D. D. Yearsly."
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 27, 1844 her only surviving child, Philofreen, also died.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=43324160 Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''May 24 2017, 23:15:33 UTC''
