1 NAME Benjamin Franklin /Whitmer/ Sr
2 GIVN Benjamin Franklin
2 SURN Whitmer
2 NSFX Sr
2 _MARNM Whitmer
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Central Cemetery Old, Central, Graham County, Arizona, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 6 MAY 1856
2 PLAC Mathias, Hardy County, West Virginia, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 22 DEC 1932
2 PLAC Central, Graham County, Arizona, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Son of David Whitmer, Sr. and Catherine Snyder.  Husband of 1. Florenia Sonifrank, 2. Sarah Hannah Wibberly, 3.  Edith Porter Kunz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Franklin Whitmer, Sr., his wife, Florenia Sonifrank, and their children, Angus, Lilly, Elmer, and Minnie came to Central, Arizona in March 1888 from West Virginia after joining the LDS Church. Benjamin was born 6 May 1856
2 CONC  in Mathias, Hardy County, West Virginia. Florenia was born the same place 21 September 1853. They were married 16 April 1878.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Arriving in Central, they purchased a ten-acre block of land and first lived in a cottonwood log cabin with a dirt floor. Soon they constructed a lumber house nearby, after selling an orchard parcel to Joseph Sowell.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1899 an epidemic of measles hit the community and Florenia died just after giving birth to a baby boy, David. The baby also died two days later, 4 May 1899.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin's oldest son, Angus, was called on a mission to the Southern States in 1899. George Coombs, Jr., a Central Ward member, was called to serve a mission in his native land, England. Before leaving, George jokingly asked Brot
2 CONC her Whitmer, "How would you like me to get you a nice little English wife?" He laughingly answered, "Yes. do."
2 CONT
2 CONT While in England, Elder Coombs met a young lady, Sarah Hannah Wibberly, who had been converted to the gospel . She was the daughter of Bryon Wibberly and Mary Ann Barnett and was born 27 August 1867 [1868] in Warwick, England. Han
2 CONC nah and her good friend Florence Skinner, worked for a rich family who were very good people. They allowed the girls to invite the Elders to their home for dinner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah was baptized 27 April 1898. When she met Elder Coombs, she was still unmarried and wanted to come to America. He told her of "Brother Whitmer" and gave her his address, and also sent her address to Brother Whitmer. This sta
2 CONC rted a correspondence which ended in marriage. They exchanged pictures, wrote letters, and after making it a matter of earnest prayer, she accepted his proposal of marriage" She said she could love anyone in the picture he sent of
2 CONC  his family. She was 33 years old but had never married although
2 CONT she had had proposals. She wanted to be married in the church and in the temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin sent her money to come to Utah. They recognized each other immediately as they met in the depot in Salt Lake City. They were married in the Salt Lake Temple 1 March 1901 and left soon for their home in Central where she b
2 CONC ecame a good mother to his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Benjamin and Hannah were born Dorothy Mary, Harry Moroni, William Bryan who died that same year, and Catherine Hannah. Benjamin worked his orchard and garden and transported his produce by team and wagon to Globe and Miami.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah was glad that her dear friend, Florence Skinner, had come to America, married Joseph W. Sowell and lived across the street from her. Hannah did some church work, but her health was not too good. Early in 1914 the doctor adv
2 CONC ised a change of climate for her, so the old home was sold and they left by train for Salt Lake City, Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1914 they settled near friends, Edwin Poole (Florence Sowell's brother-in-law) and Ira Porter.  Benjamin planted a garden and raised fruits and berries which he sold.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah's health improved for a while, then worsened and she passed away 27 June 1914. Memorial services were held for her in Willard Ward in Murray and her body was brought to Central for burial. Funeral services were held for her
2 CONC  in the Central church and she was laid to rest beside Benjamin's first wife, Florenia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family moved in with 'Auntie Flo" and "Dad" Sowell and they lived there until Benjamin bought back his old home and moved in again. A widowed house-keeper, Edith Porter Kunz was hired to come and take care of his family as she
2 CONC  had after the death of his first wife. Benjamin and Edith were married by Andrew Kimball in March 1905 [1915].
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin was an honest and religious man. He served in the Stake High Council and for a long time as the head ward teacher in Central. He died 22 December 1932. He was buried in the old Central Cemetery beside his two wives. Edith
2 CONC  remained in Central until her health became poor. She sold her property to Benjamin  Franklin Whitmer, Jr., and moved to Globe to live with her own son, Elmer.
2 CONT (A Century In Central, 1883-1983", pages 230-31.)* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/42650093/benjamin-franklin-whitmer Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 2 2023
2 CONC , 20:51:00 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
