1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ELIZABETH HALL
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Elizabeth Hall was born Nov. 10, 1816 and Chatham, Kent, England a daughter of William Hall and Mary.  Elizabeth married John Mills in 1834. She married against her parents wishes, they wanted her to wait because she was so young
2 CONC .  The young couple moved to Douglas, Isle of Mann, Ireland.  She always considered herself a true Manxsman.  They practically lived on fish and it was always her favorite food.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	The Gospel was brought to Douglas, Isle of Mann by John Taylor. John and Elizabeth were among the first converts there.  The faith of these early Saints healed their daughter Elizabeth from blindness.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	As they left to immigrate to America, Elizabeth went to bid her parents goodby.  The parents told her that they never wanted to see her again and slammed the door in her face.  They sailed on the Rochester and were blown off cour
2 CONC se.  The sea voyage took 12 weeks and they ran out of provisions.  Finally they arrived at New Orleans and took a river boat up to Nauvoo.  John Mills worked on the Nauvoo Temple and they were able to take their endowments out the
2 CONC re and witnessed it's burning.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	On the way to the Valley two of her children died.  William died of diphtheria at Winter Quarters.  Thomas died of a snake  bite while crossing the plains and was buried in an unmarked grave.  Her daughter Elizabeth was stolen by
2 CONC  the Indians and had to be bought back.  When they arrived in the Valley they rented a  small adobe cottage.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Struggling to support his family, John Mills decided to use his tin smith abilities.  He went to California to work on the gold diggings.  While soldering a keg of dynamite, it exploded and killed John.  This left Elizabeth a wid
2 CONC ow.  Elizabeth did sewing and handiwork to support herself and her remaining daughters Martha and Alice.  Her handiwork was very beautiful.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Elizabeth was very beautiful.  She never told her age and was always "just 40."  Lonely, she met and married George Rowley.  She was five years older than George.  They had two sons.  They moved to Huntsville and then to Soda Spr
2 CONC ings near Bear Lake.  George Rowley left Elizabeth and the Church. Elizabeth died the 20th of January 1900 and was buried in Soda Springs.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Residence''': Bennington, Bear Lake, Idaho, United States - June 14 1880
2 CONT ** Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-245134011-1-9972/elizabeth-rowley-born-hall-in-myheritage-family-trees MyHeritage Family Trees] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jan 21 2019
2 CONC , 3:00:55 UTC''
