1 NOTE {geni:about_me} PAUL WHITAKER FOLSOM
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Paul Whitaker Folsom was born January 22, 1900 the son of Hugh Broadbent  Folsom and Josephine Whitaker.  He had seven brothers and sisters.  In this large family he did many chores such as scrubbing the kitchen floor and doing t
2 CONC he dishes.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Paul's uncle, John Whitaker, built a cabin up Oakley Canyon.  His son Gene and Paul were close in age and one summer he went up with his Uncle's family and spent the whole summer at the cabin. Here he did a lot of fishing.  He co
2 CONC ntinued to enjoy this pastime all
2 CONT
2 CONT his life teaching most of his grandsons how to fish.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	When he was a teenager his mother gave him a birthday party. She baked a whole lemon pie for him and each of his friends.  He met Lily, his sweetheart at East High School in a German class during their Junior year.  Despite advic
2 CONC e from his parents and others he
2 CONT
2 CONT never wanted to date anyone else.  Paul was a good dancer and he and Lily enjoyed going out to Saltair to dance.  In September of 1921 they were engaged and married September 20, 1922.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Paul helped his father a lot in their fruit orchards.  He attended Utah State Agricultural college to aid him in this work. Lily and Paul's first home was in Centerville where the orchards were located.  As they were expecting th
2 CONC eir first baby they decided they
2 CONT
2 CONT needed a more dependable salary and so they moved to Salt Lake.  He had a little fruit stand and sold fruit.  For a while he sold insurance and was also a milkman for the dairy.  These depression years were a struggle financially.
2 CONC   They had three children , Vivian, Hugh Allen, and Theron Paul.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Paul got on the police force in 1938.  They were grateful for this regular salary.  He also worked as a security guard at the Coconut Grove Dance Hall later called the Rainbow.  Paul's duties on the police force were to direct th
2 CONC e safety patrol boys at all the city schools.  He would go around to the different schools and do his magic tricks and teach about safety during an assembly.  He also enjoyed being a Santa Claus at Christmas time.  He visited the
2 CONT
2 CONT children's ward at the hospitals and the Policeman's Mutual Aid Christmas Party.  After Paul retired from the police force he worked for the Utah Motor Club for several years.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Paul and Lily went on a mission to Southwest British Mission in England in 1965. Paul was the Branch President in Gloucester and Plymouth.  He and Lily would visit the inactive members and do much to build up these branches.  The
2 CONC y made many friends and would love to feed the young missionaries.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Paul loved gardening producing lots of fruits and vegetables as well as beautiful iris and roses.  He enjoyed doing macrame making gifts for the family.  He enjoyed helping Lily with the cooking and always made the morning oatmea
2 CONC l mush.  Paul has the wonderful quality of being very optimistic and happy.         	 
