1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dorothy Lenore Farrington was born at the Farrington residence, 722 South 2nd East, at 10:30 a.m., to Franklin David Farrington and Lenore Kimball.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy Lenore Hazelgren, Nolte, 89, passed away at Oak Ridge Assisted Living on July 21, 2016, in Centerville, Utah.  She was born July 3, 1927, in Salt Lake City, Utah, to Franklin D. Farrington and Lenore Kimball.  She married 
2 CONC Henry Hazelgren August 5, 1950, who passed away in 1974.  Dorothy's second husband, Rudy, started off, first, as family friends in her LDS Ward, then they became in-laws through her daughter and his son, and finally, upon becoming
2 CONC  a widower, Rudy Nolte became her husband after a little grandchild asked, 'Grandpa, why don't you marry grandma?'  Apparently they thought that was a good suggestion and they married on April 23, 1994.  Marriages to both Henry an
2 CONC d Rudy were solemnized in LDS Temples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life with Henry included managing their dairy farm in Mountain Home and Ioka, UT, but price wars, misfortune and the physical health of her husband forced them to move to Salt Lake City to be near higher levels of medical care.  T
2 CONC o help make ends meet she cleaned homes and worked at Auerbachs while Henry languished in bad health.  After Henry's death she worked at Purolator until finally marrying Rudy, her charming and happy husband who was born in Poland 
2 CONC and reared in Germany.  Rudy did everything for her and they became wonderful travelling companions going on many foreign tours both alone and with Rudy's employment.  Rudy preceded her in death in 2009.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy is survived by children: [three daughters]; step-son ___.  Her daughter, Lisa Ann Hazelgren, preceded her in death.  From these came many grandchildren and great grandchildren.  Her surviving siblings include: [one sister,
2 CONC  two brother's and three sister-in-laws].  Preceding her in death were her parents, and siblings: Cal, Donna, Marjorie, Bud, Lola and Glenn.
2 CONT
2 CONT A viewing will be held Tuesday, July 26th, at the Hillcrest 5th Ward meetinghouse at 915 E. 9045 S. in Sandy, UT, between the hours of 6 and 8 PM.  Graveside services will be held Wednesday, July 27th at 11:00 AM at Lake Hills Mem
2 CONC orial Park, 10055 South State Street, Sandy, Utah. An extra viewing has been arranged on Sunday, July 24th for special family members who will be unable to attend the Tuesday viewing and graveside services.  Although this is a hol
2 CONC iday and a Sunday, Serenity Funeral Home graciously agreed to open the Funeral Home Reception room for one hour between 5 and 6 PM.  Serenity is located at 12278 S. Lone Peak Parkway, Suite 103, Draper, UT.  All are welcome.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family wishes to express the sincere appreciation to the dedicated workers and staff who cared for Dorothy at Oak Ridge Assisted Living and her hospice at A Step Forward.  The dedication and excellence of these workers made Do
2 CONC rothy's life better through her difficult health issues. (Serenity Funeral Home, Draper, Utah website: www.serenityfhs.com)
2 CONT
2 CONT Deseret News ~ Saturday, July 30, 2016, Page B5
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy Lenore Hazelgren Nolte 1927 - 2016 Dorothy Lenore Farrington, Hazelgren, Nolte, 89, died on Thursday, July 21, 2016 in Centerville, Utah.  She was born July 3, 1927 to Franklin D. Farrington and Lenore Kimball in Salt Lake
2 CONC  City, Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 5, 1950 Dorothy married Henry LeRoy Hazelren.  They were later sealed in the Salt Lake Temple.  Henry passed away March 27, 1974 of asthma-related illnesses.  Dorothy later married Rudolph Franz Karl Nolte on April 23, 1
2 CONC 995 who later died in September 2009.  Dorothy served both of her husbands until the day each passed away, taking care of their every need.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy loved to sew and make wonderful clothing and other articles for her children.  She also loved to play the piano and travel.  Most of all, she loved people.  She was an excellent hostess.  She taught her children how to ser
2 CONC ve and treat others with love.  She loved being a mother, grandmother , and great-grandmother.  Until just days before her death she was more concerned about her children than her own (Midge) who she loved as her own.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her daughters were: Sharon Saldivar (Sam), Layton [Rose Park]; Nonie Benson (Jay), Layton, Utah; Marlene Hutchings (Mike) Draper, Utah; and Lisa Meyers who preceded her in death.  She had 18 grandchildren and numerous great grandc
2 CONC hildren.  Surviving brothers and sisters of Dorothy are: Bonnie Newman, John (Shirley) and Kenneth (Judy).  A sister-in-law Joyce (Glenn) also survives.  Preceding her in death are Dorothy's parents and brothers Cal, Bud, and Glen
2 CONC n as well as sisters Donna, Marjorie, and Lola.
