1 OCCU Married Absalom Pennington Free March 5, 1857, who was 39 years older than Annie, and theyhad 7 children.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The above photo depicts Wenlok Ammon Free and his mother, Annie Hicks Free and is from the following website:  http://thefrees.com/history/family_line/free    There was a note on the website saying that this photo 
2 CONC was from Stan Rock.  The following information about Annie is from the same website:  History of Annnie Hicks Free, written by herself, on April 9, 1931:  I was born in Barking, Essex, England, on the 8th of January 1837, the youn
2 CONC ger daughter of Daniel Hicks, a sailor, and Hannah Wenlock Hicks. I knew very little of my father's family. My mother was born of Scotch and English parents.  Father being a confirmed invalid, I had, as it were, to keep and care f
2 CONC or myself, assuming the responsibilities of a woman when I was a mere girl. As a child, I was very devout, praying and asking God for guidance and firmly believing that he would protect me from all wrong. And surely, I have been s
2 CONC aved many times from most certain evil.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was alone, or rather away from my own people at the time I first heard the Gospel and I think I loved it the first time I heard it; it seemed so quiet and pleasant to me. I embraced the Gospel and was baptized on the 17th of Jan
2 CONC uary, 1855, in the White Chapel Branch in London. Shortly after my baptism, before I had been confirmed, my relatives sent me a terrible book against the Mormons, marking it in places for me to read. The tales were so wicked, I wa
2 CONC s afraid I had done wrong and decided to ask the Lord to direct me aright. I fervently pleaded with our Father to answer my prayer that night as my confirmation was to take place the following morning.
2 CONT
2 CONT I immediately was comforted by a wonderful dream. A book (The Book of Life) was opened to me and the leaves were turned in rapid succession until the page with my record was found. On the page was my name without a mar or blemish 
2 CONC against it. A loud clear voice spoke to me saying, "This is the way, walk ye in it." I was overjoyed at this revelation and have never doubted the gospel from that time on. You may be assured I was confirmed the next day feeling p
2 CONC erfectly happy and satisfied. From then on my relatives were unkind and cruel to me. I worked very hard to obtain enough money to come to America. I would knit from early morning until evening in the London workshop.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 25th of May, 1856, I sailed for America on the ship Horizon, beginning our journey to Zion. I crossed the plains with the belated Handcart Company of Edward Martin. We underwent numerous hardships and lost many of our good 
2 CONC and faithful band on the road. I reached the valley on the last day of November 1856, with not a friend to meet me--but I am still here with the saints and many friends in the valley of the mountains. (Annie Hicks was taken to the
2 CONC  home of Brother Ellerbeck where she did housework for which she was paid 75 cents per week. She also did their knitting, sewing and embroidery work. This insert was taken from, Our Pioneer Heritage, page 184.)
2 CONT
2 CONT I was married to Absolom Pennington Free, a Patriarch of the Church on March 5, 1857, and am the mother of seven children, all of whom are living. I am also proud of my thirty-four grandchildren and the thirty great grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Annie was a gifted writer, she was the author of many beautiful poems. For many years she was secretary of the Farmers Ward Relief Society and her records were kept beautifully. A sacred hour was held in her home every Sunday to 
2 CONC which all of her grandchildren were made welcome. Always she was dressed in her best gown with a white apron. Then she would play on the organ and we would sing, after which she would read the Scriptures. These were wonderful expe
2 CONC riences to me as a child and it was then I learned to love and to read the Bible. This insert taken from, Our Pioneer Heritage, page 184.)
2 CONT
2 CONT I have been asked to relate an incident or two that might be of interest to you. One which I recall very clearly, occurred as we crossed the Platte River.  The stream was very strong and the water bitter cold, making it very hard 
2 CONC to cross. In the company was a widow with her family.  Her oldest boy, a fine young chap, had started across the river with his handcart but the current was so strong that he was borne down stream.  Seeing the boy's condition I ra
2 CONC n down the bank of the river and went out into it in time to catch the boy and his handcart. I helped the boy to shore but he was almost frozen. In the evening when the company made camp, the boy's mother was going out to gather c
2 CONC hips of wood but the boy insisted upon going himself. When he had been gone a long time, a search was made for him and he was found frozen to death with his sticks in his arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===As I Remember" Mrs. Annie Hicks Free:  by Albert Philips on August 31, 1926===
2 CONT She was a pioneer of Utah.  Seventy years of her life had been spent in the Salt Lake Valley.  When a girl of nineteen years, she left her home in England, leaving father, mother and all that she might come to the Promised Land.  
2 CONC On that long journey she endured hardships that are almost unbelievable, for she was with the last company of Handcart Pioneers under the command of Captain Edward Martin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Monday she was laid to rest in the City Cemetery.  This was Mrs. Annie Hicks Free. She had aided in every way possible to make the Salt Lake Valley and Utah what they are today and she lived to see the City, to which she came as a
2 CONC  girl, grow from a straggling hamlet to one of the most beautiful cities in the land of her adoption.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of the handcart brigade has been told and retold and yet it can never be told as it was, of the horrors of the long 1300 miles.  The story is pathetic in the extreme.  Take the history of Mrs. Free. It is similar to othe
2 CONC rs told me by the pioneers.  The company of which she was a member, when it left Florence, Nebraska on August  25, 1856, consisted of 576 persons.  They had 146 handcarts and 7 wagons.  When the company arrived in Salt Lake City o
2 CONC n November 30, 1856, after more than three months on the journey, it had been reduced to a handful of people.  Their handcarts had been left scattered along mountain and plain and many, many graves dotted the prairie and range, wh
2 CONC ere a devoted people had joined the great majority in their effort to reach their Mecca in the Mountains.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relief parties had been sent to meet the several companies of the handcart brigade and the one under Captain Martin, which was afterward known as the belated company, was reduced by death to a handful.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel W. Jones who was a member of the relief force said, "They had given up all hope, their provisions were exhausted and most of them sick and worn out. That night several members of the company died." And this was what this ag
2 CONC ed Pioneer who has just passed on went through in order that she might worship her God as her conscience dictated. Her memory will always be cherished.
