3 CITY Salt Lake City
3 STAE Utah Territory
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Utah Territorial Attorney-General, Chief Justice of Deseret; member territorial legislative council, Member Utah State Constitutional Convention, Salt Lake City, City Councilman, Public servant, financier
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Daniel Hanmer Wells''' (27 October 1814 – 24 March 1891) was an apostle of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and the third mayor of Salt Lake City, Utah Territory, United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wells married '''Eliza Rebecca Robison''' in 1837 and with her had one son Albert Emory Wells. His wife refused to accompany Wells to Utah in 1848 and later divorced him. Between 1849 and 1852, Wells married six additional wives: 
2 CONC '''Louisa Free''', with whom he had eight children; '''Martha Givens Harris''', with whom he had seven children; '''Lydia Ann Alley''', with whom he had six children; '''Susan Hannah Alley''', with whom he had four children; '''Ha
2 CONC nnah Corilla Free''', with whom he had eight children; and '''Emmeline Belos Woodward,''' with whom he had three children. Louisa Free, Hannah Free and Emmeline Woodward were all previously married and divorced or widowed. Each ha
2 CONC d one or more children whom Wells adopted and reared as his own. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the organization of the provisional government was attorney-general and later chief justice of Deseret; member first territorial legislative council many terms; member constitutional convention; mayor of city of Salt Lake 1866-
2 CONC 76; member city council until 1882, when he was disfranchised by the Edmunds law. In 1848 appointed superintendent public works; acted in that capacity in laying cornerstone of the temple 1853; superintended building of old counci
2 CONC l house, which was used as a courthouse and as the home of the University of Deseret, of which Institution he was chancellor 1869-78. Apostle and second counselor to President Brigham Young 20 years, becoming counselor to twelve a
2 CONC postles at the death of President Young. Associated with the military from the battle of Nauvoo, Illinois, till disbandment of Nauvoo Legion. He, with Charles C. Rich, organized the Utah militia, and was elected major-general by t
2 CONC he state assembly May 26, 1849, receiving the rank of lieutenant-general; under the territorial militia law was re-elected lieutenant-general April 6, 1857, and the forces under him opposed General Johnston’s advance into Salt Lak
2 CONC e valley during that and the ensuing year. During the Indian troubles in Utah and San Pete counties he took the field in defense of the settlers. Missionary to Liverpool 1864-65, and presided over the European mission. Succeeded H
2 CONC eber C. Kimball in charge of the Endowment House at Salt Lake City. Directed organization of settlements in Utah and Arizona. In December, 1884, was sent to preside over European mission, which position he held till 1887. Presiden
2 CONC t Manti temple 1888. Present at the dedication of St. George, Logan and Manti temples.
2 CONT
2 CONT First to develop coal mines in Summit county; operated lumber mills in Cottonwood canyon; manager Salt Lake nail factory; established Salt Lake City gas works, and was interested in many other business and industrial institutions.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT Wells died March 24, 1891 in Salt Lake City at the age of seventy-six. Wells' son by his wife Martha G. Harris, Heber Manning Wells, was the first governor of the state of Utah, serving from 1896 to 1905.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Marriages and Children==
2 CONT #'''Eliza Robinson''' 1835 at Commerce, Illinois. (daughter of Lewis Robinson of Salt Lake City).
2 CONT ##Albert E.
2 CONT #'''Louisa Free''' in 1849, Salt Lake City (daughter of Absalom Pennington Free and Betsy Strait of Bellville, St. Clair county, Illinois, pioneers 1848). She was born August 9, 1824.
2 CONT ##Daniel Hanmer, Jr., born November 24, 1849, married Geneva Price;
2 CONT ##Frances Louisa born March 13, 1852, married George Naylor;
2 CONT ##Rulon Seymour born July 7, 1854, married Josephine Eliza Beatie;
2 CONT ##Emeline Young born April 13, 1857;
2 CONT ##Eliza Free born October 3, 1859;
2 CONT ##Clara Ellen born October 23, 1862, married William S. Hedges;
2 CONT ##Melvin Dickenson born July 31, 1867, married Elizabeth Ann Young.
2 CONT #'''Martha Harris''' in 1849.
2 CONT ##Martha Deseret, married Charles Read;
2 CONT ##Emily, married Heber Jeddy Grant;
2 CONT ##Heber Manning, married Elizabeth Beatje, married Theresa Clawson, married Emily Katz;
2 CONT ##Joseph Smith, married Annie Sears, married Mary Lovell;
2 CONT ##Edna, married Thomas W. Sloan;
2 CONT ##Briant Harris, married Mary Jennings.
2 CONT #'''Lydia Ann Alley''' in 1852 (daughter of George Alley and Ann Symons).
2 CONT ##Catherine;
2 CONT ##Lucy Ann;
2 CONT ##Wilford;
2 CONT ##Arthur D.;
2 CONT ##Mary Minerva, married Orson F. Whitney;
2 CONT ##Louis Robison, married Inga Hansen.
2 CONT #'''Susan H. Alley''' in 1852 (daughter of George Alley and Ann Symons).
2 CONT ##Annette, married H. L. A. Culmer;
2 CONT ##George A.;
2 CONT ##Stephen;
2 CONT ##Charles H., married Susan Riter.
2 CONT #'''Hannah C. Free''' (daughter of Absalom Pennington Free and Betsy Strait).
2 CONT ## Abbie C., married Seymour B. Young;
2 CONT ##Junius Free born June 1, 1854, married Helena Forbes;
2 CONT ##Gershom Britain Finley born November 1864, married Nellie Sheets, married Maud Freeze;
2 CONT ##Victor Pennington;
2 CONT ##Luna Pamela, died child;
2 CONT ##Brigham, died child;
2 CONT ##Ephraim, diec child;
2 CONT ##Preston, died child.
2 CONT #'''Emmeline B. Woodward''' (Whitney) in 1852.
2 CONT ##Emmie born September 10, 1853, died 1877;
2 CONT ##Annie, married John Q. Cannon;
2 CONT ##Louie, died aged 18.
2 CONT ##Emmeline B. Woodward, married Newell K. Whitney (first husband).
2 CONT ###Isabel, married Septimus A. Sears;
