2 CONT                         LETTER BY JOHN D. LEE                 Sugarhouse Ward, Salt Lake City, Dec 11, 1875 My Dear kind and affectionate Children:        Your truly interesting letter dated November 22nd was received and handed t
2 CONC o by thismail, its contents was duly appreciated and I hasten to acknowledge receipt of the same by return mail.  I was glad to learn that you were all well and that general prosperity prevails, that you were blessed with peace an
2 CONC d a bountiful harvest.  Your explanation and the purity of your motives in writing as you did was satisfactory to me.  I was not vexed at you for writing as you did, but to the contrary I was pleased with the plain honest way that
2 CONC  you represented things to be, they were the simple facts with evasion, which was the right way to do.  It enables me to know the true state of affairs and gives me the chance to correct anything that should not be. (Part is torn 
2 CONC away here.)  I have not done so througha wicked or malicious intent; I have only intended to check the progress of evil influences, that might have crept in unobserved slyly into the mist of my family, to divide our interest and t
2 CONC hereby blast our prosperity.  My interest at theDell and my ranch in Arizona; nearly all our possessions lies at those two places, and any move made to neglect or weaken our interest there I considered very short sighted and you (
2 CONC Part is gone) insist upon anything that I did not believeto be right.  I have a letter from Joseph, two from Willard and either 2 or 3 from your sister Nancy and Mother.  Some were as far back as Oct. 10. all of whom acknowledged 
2 CONC receiving letters before they wrote to me saying that they had been waiting to write to learn my address, the last letter dated November 10 stating that your mother intended to start the next day and would be here within 3 weeks, 
2 CONC unavoidable hindrance considered.  That time is past.  I have been very sicksince I last wrote to (Part is gone here) here with sentences ranging from two to 39 years unimagination--of element to surround a sick man especially one
2 CONC  of my temperment, confusion is and always was a Hell to me, in my well eays; and wasit not for the strict rule of discipline it would have been worse.  My clothing is here, are all worn out, and the cold pinches me pretty hard, w
2 CONC hich I think is the cause of my feeble health.  I have no comforter, my feet is cold, all thewhile; this is a colder climate than the Dell.  I have but little appetite to eat.  I have had a little money from one and another that h
2 CONC as called to see me, which has enabled me to get a little medicine and nourishment. Bp. Dame is not inthe same aprtment with me, yet through the courtesy of the warden he has been allowed to see me and has been very kind and has p
2 CONC rocured medicine and nourishment for me in my sickness.  The court is in session here. Judge Sutherland was to see Dame yesterday and will start to Beaver this morning to attend court and try to bring on a trial for Dame etc.,  I 
2 CONC have no knowledge when I will get a hearing, perhaps not till after the action of congress. (Part is torn away,  The ending is complete as follows:) Much anxiety is manifest by both parties in the next presidential election. The P
2 CONC resident's message is much speculated upon.        We had a month of stormy weather,  the snow is very deep in the mountains but little in the valley.        I can truly and justly be said of me that I am a man of sorrow and acqua
2 CONC inted with grief.        Remember me kindly to all enquiring friends.  Kiss little John Doyle for me.  Write often and receive the confidenceand love of your true and affectionate father, though in tribulation deep.
2 CONT                                         J. D. Lee To Lehi and Amorah Smithson  (Rachel Amorah)
2 CONT
