2 CONT In the year 1842, John Doyle Lee and Levi Stewart were sent fromNauvoo on a mission to the South.  When the Saints were driven fromNauvoo, the Youngs were in the company of John Doyle Lee, leaving inFebruary 1846. They made their 
2 CONC weary wayacross the state of Iowa toWinter Quarters. Here on February 27, 1847, Polly, then 30 years ofage, and Lovina, age 27, were married by Brigham Young to John DoyleLee. Delegated to stay and raise food crops for the Saints 
2 CONC as theycame along,the Lee family moved eighteen miles northwest of WinterQuarters to a place known as Summer Quarters where they raised morethan 4,000 bushels of corn the first season. Deposits of coal and ironhad been discovered 
2 CONC in southern Utah. If the kingdom were to become areality they would need to develop these minerals, thereby savingtransporting iron products from the east. The call to make settlementsfor this purpose came in 1850. None wanted to 
2 CONC leave their newly madehomes in the Valley, but the call was made and John Doyle was one whoanswered. He left most of his family in Cottonwood but took Polly andLovina with him.
2 CONT  Quoting from his journal: "Wed. Dec. 11, John D. Lee started for IronCounty with two wagons, four yoke of cattle, three cows and heifersalso one carriage and two horses, ... two teamsters, 400 pounds offlour to each person and th
2 CONC en some 2200 pounds extra; 100 poundsgroceries, 1 barrel of pork and one each of crout, pickles, beans,peas and dried fruit, 30 women, 119 men, 18 children, total of 167."Our  [p.253] The county was named Iron County and the first
2 CONC  settlementwas Parowan. It took five weeks to travel 250 miles, the companyarriving the last of January 1851. The road was unmarked andundeveloped, thus creating a hazardous and painful journey. The wagonswere overloaded and the t
2 CONC eams poorly fed. In the buggyin which Pollyand Lovina rode was a small stove with the pipe extending through atin reinforcement in the top. For a stove many of them used a hot rockor a bucket with sand in the bottom, putting in li
2 CONC ve coals and ashesas they left the campeach day. On arriving they made themselves ascomfortable as possible. On March 19, 1851, Lovina gave birth to herfirst child, the first to be born in the town, a son whom she namedJohn David,
2 CONC  for her husband, her father and brother. Polly's firstchild was born April 24, 1851, a daughter whom she named Elizabeth forher mother. Lovina's second child was born November 11, 1852, atParowan but Polly's second, a son named J
2 CONC ames Young, was born at oldFort Harmony. Polly and Lovinaeach had three children. The latterones being born at old Fort Harmony, it is presumed they were livingat that place. The next we learn about Polly and Lovina, they arelivin
2 CONC g at Washington near St. George in 1861 where their husband ownedland, a house, two milk cows and a supply of grape cuttings and fruittrees to plant. The sisters lived in one big room, partitioned to giveeach her own place. Accust
2 CONC omed to being left to manage for themselvesfor long periods, they made out. Polly being the weaker of the twoperformed the household tasks while Lovina worked in the fields, aidedby her ten-year-old son David, and Polly's son Jame
2 CONC s. They managed toeke out a living. Ten years later, after their husband had set up theferry across the Colorado River for settlers moving further south, wefind Lovina and Polly living at Skutujpas, south and east of Kanab.
2 CONT  FURTHER_READING: General Doucments "Mountain Meadow Massacre.doc"
2 CONT  FURTHER_READING: General Documents "John Doyle Lee.doc"
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Other wives: 1 - Aggatha Ann WOOLSEY   (24 July 1833) date married             2 - Nancy BEAN (5 Feb 1845)             3 - Louisa FREE (19 Apr 1845) divorced & married Daniel H. W e lls             4 - Sarah Caroline WILLIAMS (19 
2 CONC Apr 1845)5 - Abigail WOOLSEY (3 May 1845) Mother of Aggatha, Rachel , E moline             6 - Rachel Andora WOOLSEY (3 May 1845)             7 - Polly Ann WORKMAN - (fall 1845) she went back to live w i th                 her bro
2 CONC ther and married a man named Mr. Bennett in Iow a             8 - Martha Elizabeth BERRY (summer or fall 1845)             9 - Delethia MORRIS (fall or winter 1845) married Mr. Mill e r later            10 - Nancy Ann VANCE - (fal
2 CONC l of 1845) married William Words w orth            11 - Emoline Vaughn WOOLSEY - (December 21, 1846)            12 - Nancy Gibbons ARMSTRONG - (Feb. 27, 1847)            13 - Mary Vance (Polly) YOUNG - (27 Feb, 1847)            14
2 CONC  - Lavina YOUNG (sister toPolly) - (same date)            15 - Mary Leah GROVES - (1853 joined family)            16 - Mary Ann WILLIAMS - (1856) married his oldest son Alma l a ter            17 - Emma BATCHELOR (7 Jan 1858)     
2 CONC        18 - Terressa Morse CHAMBERLAIN (18 Mar 1859)            19 - Ann GORDGE - (probably 1865)
