1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Wikipedia Biographical Summary:==
2 CONT
2 CONT "...'''Brigham Young '''(June 1, 1801 – August 29, 1877) was an American leader in the Latter Day Saint movement and was the president of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints (LDS Church) from 1847 until his death. Youn
2 CONC g was the founder of Salt Lake City and the first governor of the Utah Territory, United States, and is the namesake of Brigham Young University..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Young was born to a farming family in Whitingham, Vermont and worked as a traveling carpenter and blacksmith, among other trades. Young first married in 1824 to Miriam Angeline Works. Though he had converted to the Methodist f
2 CONC aith in 1823, Young was drawn to Mormonism after reading the Book of Mormon shortly after its publication in 1830. He officially joined the new church in 1832 and traveled to Upper Canada as a missionary. After his first wife died
2 CONC  in 1832, Young joined many Mormons in establishing a community in Kirtland, Ohio. Young was ordained a member of the original Quorum of the Twelve Apostles in 1835, and he assumed a leadership role within that organization in tak
2 CONC ing Mormonism to the United Kingdom and organizing the exodus of Latter Day Saints from Missouri in 1838..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Young was ordained President of the Church in December 1847..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...As colonizer and founder of Salt Lake City, Young was appointed the territory's first governor and superintendent of American Indian affairs by President Millard Fillmore..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Young was a polygamist, marrying a total of 55 wives, 54 of them after he converted to become a Latter Day Saint. The policy was difficult for many in the church. Young stated that upon being taught about plural marriage, "It 
2 CONC was the first time in my life that I desired the grave." By the time of his death, Young had 56 children by 16 of his wives; 46 of his children reached adulthood..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Sources have varied on the number of Young's wives, due to differences in what scholars have considered to be a "wife"..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Young did not live with a number of his wives or publicly hold them out as wives, which has led to confusion on the number and identities. This is in part due to the complexity of how wives were identified in the Mormon societ
2 CONC y at the time. If a woman was married and her husband died, she often married another man in proxy of her former husband..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...[Young died in] Salt Lake City at 4:00pm on August 29, 1877..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...His last words were "Joseph, Joseph..." upon anticipation of being reunited with his predecessor, Joseph Smith. On September 2, 1877, Young's funeral was held in the Tabernacle with an estimated 12,000 to 15,000 people in atte
2 CONC ndance..."
2 CONT
2 CONT >''SOURCE'': Wikipedia contributors, 'Brigham Young', ''Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia'', 19 April 2011, 21:14 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Brigham_Young&oldid=424917973> [accessed 29 April 2011] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Additional Resources:==
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.pbs.org/weta/thewest/people/s_z/young.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.lds.org/library/pio_sto/Pioneer_Trail/41_Brigham_Young.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Marriages:==
