1 NOTE Young was the American religious leader and colonizer of Utah, second 
2 CONT president of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter Day Saints (Mormons). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Young spent his boyhood in the farm country of Vermont and western New 
2 CONT York and received only two months of formal schooling. He worked as a 
2 CONT carpenter, glazier, and journeyman painter. In 1829 he settled in the town 
2 CONT of Mendon, New York, where he met a brother and disciple of Joseph Smith, 
2 CONT founder and prophet of the Mormons. Smith's brother converted Young, 
2 CONT previously a Methodist, to Mormonism, and on April 14, 1832, he was 
2 CONT baptized and confirmed in the church. Young quickly distinguished himself 
2 CONT as a preacher and evangelist in the area around Mendon; within the year he 
2 CONT was ordained an elder. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1833 and 1836, Young's fame and stature within the Mormon movement 
2 CONT rapidly increased. About 1833 he organized a group of Mormons in the 
2 CONT eastern states and led them to Kirtland, Ohio, where Joseph Smith had 
2 CONT established headquarters. At Kirtland, Young met Smith for the first time. 
2 CONT Impressed by Young's zeal and persuasive powers, Smith sent him into the 
2 CONT surrounding states and Canada on missionary assignments. When, in 1835, 
2 CONT the Mormons created a Quorum, or Council, of Twelve Apostles with powers 
2 CONT second only to those of Smith, Young was named one of the apostles. In 
2 CONT 1836 he was elected president of the Quorum. He was a strong figure in the 
2 CONT movement during the period of persecution, climaxed (1838) by the 
2 CONT migration to Hancock County, Illinois, and the establishment there of 
2 CONT Nauvoo as the new center of Mormonism. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1839 to 1841 Young worked with the Mormon mission in Liverpool, 
2 CONT England, preaching and distributing religious literature; he arranged for 
2 CONT the emigration of about 70,000 converts from Europe to America. He 
2 CONT returned to the U.S. in 1841 and for several years served as a missionary 
2 CONT in the eastern states. After Joseph Smith was shot and killed by a lynch 
2 CONT mob in 1844, Young was elected acting president of the Mormon church and 
2 CONT henceforth was its leader. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of sentiment against their group in Illinois, Young and his 
2 CONT colleagues decided to leave Nauvoo. In 1846-47 he organized and supervised 
2 CONT the migration of close to 5000 Mormons across the Great Plains and the 
2 CONT Rocky Mountains into the arid Great Basin. There, in Great Salt Lake 
2 CONT Valley, he founded (July 1847) Great Salt Lake City. On December 5, 1847, 
2 CONT he was formally elected head of the Mormon church. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Young's autocratic leadership Salt Lake City and the surrounding 
2 CONT region soon became the Zion, or promised land, that the Mormons had long 
2 CONT sought. His followers build extensive irrigation projects; developed 
2 CONT farms, small businesses, and cooperative stores; and set up a legislature, 
2 CONT public school, and two institutions of higher learning. In 1850 the U.S. 
2 CONT Congress enacted legislation establishing the region, previously known as 
2 CONT the state of Deseret, as the territory of Utah. Young was made territorial 
2 CONT governor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1852, Young publicly endorsed the doctrine of polygamy, basing 
2 CONT his pronouncement on a revelation said to have been experienced by Joseph 
2 CONT Smith in 1843. His open advocacy of the doctrine disturbed the federal 
2 CONT government and the non-Mormon residents of Utah. Finally, in 1857, 
2 CONT President James Buchanan appointed a new territorial governor. Young 
2 CONT refused to relinquish his post, and when rumors of an armed Mormon 
2 CONT rebellion reached Washington, the president sent federal troops to Utah. 
2 CONT Hostilities were averted, largely as a result of Young's statesmanship, 
2 CONT and the new governor was installed without incident. Nonetheless, as 
2 CONT president of the church, Young continued to play a dominant role in Utah. 
2 CONT In 1871 he was indicted on a polygamy charge but was not convicted. Young 
2 CONT is believed to have married 27 times and was survived by 17 wives and 57 
2 CONT children.
