1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/101428277/elijah-burt Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 28 2022, 6:27:52 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Elijah Burt (1742–1820) was a prominent figure in Longmeadow history, known both for his artistic contributions as a master gravestone carver and for his association with the town's oldest surviving residence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Civic Roles: Elijah Burt was a noted gravestone carver in Longmeadow; he even carved the headstone for his brother, Jonathan.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Elijah Burt House (201 Chestnut Street)'''
2 CONT Located in East Longmeadow, this home is a central landmark for the family: 
2 CONT Age: Built between 1720 and 1740, it is believed to be the oldest house in East Longmeadow.
2 CONT Underground Railroad Station: In the mid-19th century, the house gained fame as a station on the Underground Railroad. While it bears Elijah's name due to family ownership, the most active abolitionist work occurred later under ow
2 CONC ner Randolph Stebbins.
2 CONT Secret Features: The house contains a secret passage and a false closet that led to a concealed staircase in the basement where runaway slaves were hidden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Escape Route: A bricked-up exit in the cellar likely provided a direct route for slaves to enter or exit the home under cover of darkness.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career as a Gravestone Carver
2 CONT Elijah was a skilled stonecutter, working primarily with the distinct red sandstone found in East Longmeadow. 
2 CONT Instagram
2 CONT Instagram
2 CONT  +1
2 CONT Artistic Legacy: His work is recognizable throughout the Connecticut River Valley for its high-relief carvings and durability.
2 CONT Notable Stones: He carved many headstones in the Longmeadow Cemetery, as well as in nearby Somers and Agawam. His own headstone in Longmeadow serves as a final example of the craft he practiced for decades
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Underground Railroad Connection'''
2 CONT While the Elijah Burt House is often associated with abolitionism, the most active period of slave harboring occurred in the 1850s under a later owner, Randolph Stebbins. Stebbins was motivated by his own radical Christian beliefs
2 CONC  and local abolitionist networks rather than Quaker membership.
