1 FAMS @F6000000006452668671@
1 RFN geni:6000000006452668666
1 SUBM @S6000000000834094168@
1 NOTE Birthdate also listed as 28 Apr 1830.
2 CONT This information comes from our findings in a book entitled "Montana History" with a page that my mother copied in 1964 in the Spokane, Washington City Library.  Under the Jefferson County where the Colliers lived the book has an 
2 CONC entry for Irvin Collier P. O. Beaver Creek.  The entry states that he was born August 28, 1830 Clay Co. Kentucky, that his father was a farmer and speculator by occupation.  That he, Irvin left Kentucky in  1843-44 and traveled th
2 CONC rough Illinois to Iowa staying in Iowa about 18 months working on a farm not far from Winterset.  From there he went to Nebraska and on to Missouri and Kansas only stopping for a brief time in these states.  From Kansas he went to
2 CONC  Memphis, Tennessee returning again to Nebraska and from there to Indiana.  He then goes to Denver, Colorado in 1859 staying there until 1864 working in the mines prospecting and freighting.  After starting back to the States he i
2 CONC s inducted into the Union forces and taken to Vicksburg.  In the spring of 1864 he is going west again and is at Alder Gulch for a couple of winters again mining and hauling timber.  From Alder Gulch he goes to Last Chance Gulch i
2 CONC n 1866.  He took up 160 acres of land on the Missouri River where he resides in Montana.  The book states he is married to Mary Alice Mills, a native of Utah, March 7, 1871 and at the time the book is published the father of seven
2 CONC  children, David, Andrew Irvin, James Edward, John, Warren Word, Thomas Franklin and Charles.  He signed somed deeds to his land in Montana from a Mr. Petitte of California with an X which shows James Ervin didn't read or write.
2 CONT Teresa Collier Wallace
2 CONT 30205 Chualar Canyon Rd.
2 CONT Chualar, Ca. 93925
1 CHAN
2 DATE 07 NOV 2020
3 TIME 04:13:23
