1 NAME Lily Ethel /Allen/
2 GIVN Lily Ethel
2 SURN Allen
2 _MARNM Allen
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 13 MAR 1901
2 PLAC Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 JUN 1996
2 PLAC Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT 	I was the first child of my parents, M. C. John Allen and Mina Ethel Livesey and my father named me Lily Ethel Allen.  I was born the 13 of March 1901 at my parents home which was a three-room apartment that my grandfather had bu
2 CONC ilt for them at the rear of his
2 CONT
2 CONT home.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	When I was about two years old my mother almost died with diphtheria and I stayed with my mother's parents until she completely recovered.  It was at this time that my Grandfather Allen took me for long rides in his horse-drawn b
2 CONC uggy while he inspected fruit
2 CONT
2 CONT orchards.  It was on one of these trips that Grandpa got me the little red glass cup with my name written on it.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Because we didn't have any playmates my brother Theron and I were very close.  For almost every dress I had Mamma made a duplicate dress for my doll; for Theron's boy doll too.  He played with his boy doll right along with me whi
2 CONC ch bothered Papa very much. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 	When I was about eight years old Mr. and Mrs. Nutt moved into the duplex next door.  Mr. Nutt asked Mamma if he could take me to Sunday School.  It was then I learned the children's version of the Gospel.  I was soon going to chu
2 CONC rch, paying my pennies for tithing,
2 CONT
2 CONT coaxing my parents to go to church with me, and begging to be baptized.  By the time I was ten, I worried about not being baptized so much that I began to walk in my sleep.  My parents decided that they had better do everything th
2 CONC ey could to help me so on March 4, 1911 Mamma and I took the street car and went to the baptismal font at the rear of the Tabernacle and I was finally baptized.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	When I was fourteen Papa came home with a violin in a heavy black wooden case.  My teacher said I had talent so my parents let nothing interfere with my practicing.  About this same time I got a job at Schwar's bakery each weeken
2 CONC d.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Paul and I met our first year at East High School and started dating the next year.  At this time Paul was always near and considered me his girl.  I thought he should take out other girls but he didn't pay any attention to these
2 CONC  suggestions.  He dated me so often I didn't get a chance to go out with other boys much.  It didn't matter because Paul and I had lots of fun together.  We went to Saltair a lot to dance.  One day in September 1921 Paul met me
2 CONT
2 CONT after work.  As we got off the street car he pulled an engagement ring out of his pocket.  We were married on the 20th of September 1922 in the Salt Lake Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	We lived in Centerville for over a year where Paul worked in his father's orchards and then we moved to Salt Lake.  I was expecting our first baby.  She was so welcome.  We named her Vivian Nadine.  We were still living in the sa
2 CONC me place when our second child was born,
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Allen.  In 1929 our third baby was born.  We named him Theron Paul.  We still kept up with all our friends, old and new.  We formed a club which met once a month.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	At the end of a trip back east visiting my brother Theron, our car slid off a winding road into a telephone pole.  Besides my ankle being smashed a small bone in my leg was broken.  I was in the Wheeling Hospital for six weeks be
2 CONC fore I was able to return to Utah. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I never expected to be well enough to do much, but I continued to pray that I would be able to raise my three babies.  The evening before I was to go to the hospital another time, the block teachers called.  Paul asked if they wou
2 CONC ld administer to me and they did.  The main thing Brother Calderwell said was "Sister, I feel impressed to tell you that your wish will be granted."  We all noticed that especially.  After they left we talked about the blessing, a
2 CONC nd then right out of the clear sky I began to sing, "All That Was Promised the Saints will be Given."  At our final visit to the doctor he kept saying, "You hardly limp at all, it's a miracle."
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Vivian married John Merle Taylor, Hugh married Helen Soderberg, and Theron married Judy Folsom.  After our children were grown, life kept busy as I cared for Mamma who moved into our home with us for a time.  I also tended Vivi's
2 CONC  children as she went back to work full time.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In the summer of 1965 we were called to serve a mission to the Southwest British Mission.  We served in Gloucester and Plymouth. Paul was Branch President.  We were really missionaries working for our Heavenly Father.  We attende
2 CONC d all our meetings, we substituted for all absent teachers, gave talks and prayers, bore our testimonies, and even tended babies so parents could listen.  We called on the inactive members besides all the active members many times
2 CONC .  We found ourselves custodians of the big old house that was
2 CONT
2 CONT our chapel.  By increasing attendance we were able to get a new chapel built in Gloucester otherwise the branch would have been dissolved.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	I always put up fruit, pickles and jellies as the different things came in season.  Paul had a wonderful vegetable garden and we canned a lot of it.  Along with many beautiful roses and iris I also got plants and slips and starte
2 CONC d my miniature rose garden that we always enjoyed.  I had a whole border of pinkies that I slipped from Loran's garden (Judy's father).  In 1981 we had a fire in our home.  In the middle of the night the smoke alarm woke us.  All 
2 CONC the main floor with most of our furnishings were burned.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	I am so thankful to my Father in Heaven for my grand family.  I have prayed for all of them to live close to the Lord.  I still have my strong testimony that the Gospel is true.  That helps me over the rough places and gives me h
2 CONC ope for the rest of my life.  I am so thankful for Paul.  He is so kind and dear to me.	
